hello today we are going to talk about  normalizing the table of expression  rna scene data has been shown to have  skewed distributions on equal variances  and extreme values  most statistical tests including ones  that we use in our analysis have an  assumption of normality the three  aforementioned issues violate these  assumptions  there are a number of normalization  approaches that may be used a standard  approach includes conversion of values  to log scale and adjusting the  distribution to account for technical  variability of low expressed genes  aligning the mean of distribution  between all samples in a set  on the t bioinfo platform both of these  can be done using the quantile  normalization utility  this utility can be found on the main  areas of analysis page  quantile normalization and other  normalization procedures are important  to transform distributions both between  and amongst different samples to have  the same distribution  this removes unwanted technical  variation that could cause perceived  differences between samples when there  are in fact no differences in terms of  the biology  quantile normalization is a technique of  mapping original values the  corresponding quantile of a standard  distribution  here an estimation of the sequencing  depth of an experiment is determined by  an upper quartile of his counts  comparing quantile values instead of raw  values will eliminate differences solely  rooted in the in the technical variation  such as the amount of rna sequenced  sequencing machine type or even  unexplained differences that arise  between different technicians following  the same protocol in addition while  normalizing data we will use a log  transformation that makes it easier to  interpret differences in gene expression  to use both normalization and log normal  transformation of the expression data  select quantile normalization  after start  and  set the following settings  the transform table to ln should be s  and threshold should be 5.  now we are ready to download the table  output and compare expression between  fbkm and normalized data  a good way to compare normalized and  adjusted data is to take a few samples  and visualize all gene expression  numbers as a box plot  this method of data visualization  popular to summarize data shows the  maximum minimum and mean values for each  sample  while displaying the interquartile  ranges of upper and lower interquartile  ranges thus we can see some detail but  not too much to lose the trend  you can prepare the box plots in excel  to see how it can show us a comparison  between fbk output and normalized data  the first figure shows the majority of  values that is not seen at all because  there is a gap between the majority low  levels and some outlier high values in  the selected samples  after normalization this gap disappears  placing most of the data into a more  easily distinguishable scale  in fact it shows the variance of data  that is needed for a comprehensive  comparison for example if we prepare a  box plot of all the samples we will see  that some of them are outliers  the outlier status of the sample is not  by level of expression of any single  gene  it shows that the variance of this data  set is different from the others the  mean of the sample is just a little  higher than the rest but the distance  between the mean value of the lower  interquartile range and the maximum of  the top interquartile range is much  bigger than the rest of the samples  to learn more about normalization you  can visit lesson number 12 under  transcriptomics on learn.omicslogic.com  portal
 

